.TH CCOL_LOG_ERROR 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_log_error \- emit a log line at ERROR level with a backtrace
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

ccol_log_error(clog lg, const char *fmt, ...);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Writes a log line at CLOG_ERROR level.  It captures __FILE__,
__LINE__ and __func__ by itself.  After it writes the structured log
line, it appends a stack backtrace as continuation lines that a tab
indents:

  ts=... level=ERROR src=file.c:42 func=handle msg="db timeout"
  \et#0 ./myapp(handle+0x1a) [0x7f...]
  \et#1 ./myapp(main+0x42) [0x7f...]

A continuation line does not start with "ts=".  A log aggregator can
therefore group it with the ERROR record before it.

The library skips backtrace frames 0 and 1, which belong to the logger
itself.  Frame #0 is therefore the caller of ccol_log_error.  The depth
of a backtrace is capped at 64 frames.

A backtrace needs the binary to be linked with -rdynamic, so that the
library can resolve symbols.  The capture itself can fail.  It fails on
a platform with no execinfo.h, and on a transient allocation failure
while the library resolves symbol names.  The logger still writes the
log line normally.  It then appends one "\et#error backtrace
unavailable" continuation line, in place of the usual lines for each
frame.  The omission is therefore visible, and not silent.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
lg     Logger handle (clog).
fmt    printf-style format string.
\&...    Format arguments (optional).
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
ccol_log_error does NOT stop the process.  Use ccol_log_fatal when you
want the process to stop.
The shared write lock also protects the write of the backtrace.  The
backtrace and the log line before it therefore always appear together.
That holds when several threads log at the same time.
Memory pressure can last for a time.  The content of a record can also
leave little room before the internal buffer cap.  A single backtrace
frame that the library cannot format is then omitted.  A shorter frame
that follows it can still go out.  The library never writes a frame
that is incomplete, so every line that it writes is a complete,
well-formed record.  When not one frame fits at all, one "\et#error
backtrace unavailable" continuation line takes their place.  It is the
same marker that the library uses when the capture itself fails.  In
CLOG_FMT_JSON mode the library can fail to embed the backtrace at all
under such pressure.  A "bt_error":"unavailable" key then takes the
place of "bt", so the omission is visible and not silent.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_log_error(lg, "failed to open %s: %s", path, strerror(errno));
ccol_log_error(lg, "unexpected state %d in FSM", state);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_log_alert (3),
.BR ccol_log_fatal (3),
.BR ccol_log_warn (3),
.BR clog_open_fd_mp (3)
